# ====================================================== NARRATIVE AND POETRY
ch = p.chapter("narrative-poetry", "Narrative technique and advanced poetry")
L = lesson(ch, "narrative-techniques", "Narrative techniques: voice, time and irony",
    ["Analyse unreliable narration, free indirect speech, flashback and foreshadowing", "Explain how structure shapes meaning", "Comment on an original prose extract with precision"],
    [("definition", "Voice", "An **unreliable narrator** gives a view that the reader learns to doubt (through bias, self-deception, or lack of knowledge). **Free indirect speech** blends narrator and character voice: ‘He would never forgive them. Never.’ without ‘he thought’. **Stream of consciousness** presents thoughts as they flow. A **frame narrative** places one story inside another."),
     ("retenir", "Time and structure", "**Flashback** moves back in time, **foreshadowing** hints at future events, **chronology** can be linear or fragmented. **Pace** is controlled by summary (fast) and scene (slow). **Irony** arises when meaning differs from appearance: **verbal** (saying the opposite), **situational** (outcome contrary to expectation), **dramatic** (reader knows more than character)."),
     ("methode", "Commenting on technique", "Name the technique, quote briefly, explain **how the reader’s understanding is shaped**, and link to theme. Ask: Why this narrator? Why this order? What is withheld, and why?"),
     ("pieges", "Avoid", "- Calling every first-person narrator unreliable: look for evidence of doubt.\n- Identifying a device without discussing its effect.\n- Confusing the author’s views with the narrator’s.")],
    [("Example 1 — Unreliable narration", "Original extract: ‘I have always been the fairest man in this district. If the tenants whisper that I raised the rent twice this year, it is because they cannot count. Mr Fon, who I will not name again, has the nerve to say that I am hard.’ Comment on the narrator.",
      ["Self-praise at the start is a claim the reader must test.", "He blames tenants for ‘not counting’ and avoids denying the rent rises.", "The reader infers that he is defensive, self-deceiving and probably unfair: an unreliable narrator."], "He undermines himself; the reader doubts his account."),
     ("Example 2 — Foreshadowing", "Original sentence: ‘That morning the cock crowed three times before anyone moved, and Mama said nothing about the cracked mirror.’ What might the sentence foreshadow?",
      ["Unusual signs (the cock, the cracked mirror) are the kind of detail linked to bad omens.", "Mama’s silence suggests worry.", "The reader expects misfortune."], "It hints at coming misfortune.")],
    [M("An unreliable narrator is one whose account", "The reader learns to doubt", ["Is always correct", "Is told in the third person only", "Has no opinions"], "Evidence of bias or self-deception."),
     TF("Foreshadowing hints at future events.", True, "Definition."),
     MT("Match the term to the meaning.", [("Flashback", "Return to an earlier time"), ("Situational irony", "Outcome opposite to expectation"), ("Frame narrative", "A story inside another story"), ("Free indirect speech", "Blend of narrator and character voice")], "Standard terms."),
     M("Dramatic irony occurs when", "The reader knows more than the character", ["The narrator lies", "The writer rhymes", "The text is short"], "Gap in knowledge."),
     O("Original sentence: ‘The doctor assured everyone that the bridge was perfectly safe, and then went home by the long road.’ Explain the irony.", "The doctor’s words claim safety, but his own choice to avoid the bridge shows he does not trust it. This is verbal and situational irony: what he says contradicts what he does. It invites the reader to distrust the reassurance.", "Irony identified (1); contradiction (1); effect (1); quotation (1)."),
     O("Original extract: ‘My brother says I am stubborn. He forgets who paid for his schooling, who found him the job, who sat up through his fever. Stubborn! I have simply never been wrong.’ Write a paragraph on how the writer presents the narrator.", "The narrator seems proud and self-justifying. The list of favours reveals resentment, while the exclamation ‘Stubborn!’ shows irritation. The final claim ‘never been wrong’ is absolute and comic, making him unreliable and slightly blind to his own faults. The rhythm of repeated ‘who’ builds accusation.", "Tone identified (1); repetition/list (1); absolute claim (1); effect of unreliability (1).")],
    [("Stream of consciousness presents", "Thoughts as they flow", ["Rhymed couplets", "Stage directions", "Summaries only"], "A narrative technique."),
     ("A flashback is", "A shift to an earlier time", ["A look to the future", "A rhyme", "A prop"], "Backwards in time."),
     ("Verbal irony means", "Saying the opposite of what is meant", ["Telling the truth", "Repeating", "Describing nature"], "Gap between words and meaning."),
     ("Pace is slowed by", "Detailed scene", ["Summary", "Silence only", "Blank pages"], "Scene slows, summary speeds up."),
     ("Foreshadowing creates", "Expectation or suspense", ["Boredom", "Rhyme", "Comedy only"], "Hints at what will happen.")],
    (table(["Technique", "Effect"], [["Unreliable narrator", "Doubt and irony"], ["Flashback", "Context, depth"], ["Foreshadowing", "Suspense"], ["Free indirect speech", "Close to character"]], 440, 13, 32),
     "Narrative techniques and their effects.", "A table of four narrative techniques and their effects."),
    [NOTE, "Terminology is standard; check whether the syllabus names specific techniques."])

L = lesson(ch, "poetry-comparison", "Unseen poetry and comparing poems",
    ["Compare two unseen poems on shared theme", "Use comparative connectives and structure", "Write an integrated comparison"],
    [("definition", "Why compare?", "Comparison shows how **different poets treat a similar theme** through **form, imagery, sound, tone and speaker**. You look for **similarities** and **differences** and explain why they matter. A comparison is not two separate commentaries: keep both poems in view in every paragraph (the **integrated** method)."),
     ("methode", "Structure", "Introduction: name the shared theme and state the main contrast. Then take **points** (for example attitude to the city, imagery, form, ending) and compare both poems within each. Use connectives: *similarly, in contrast, whereas, both, by comparison, however*. End with a judgement about the poems’ effects."),
     ("retenir", "What to compare", "**Speaker and situation**; **tone** and any shift; **imagery** (type and effect); **form** (stanza, rhyme, rhythm, line length); **sound**; **ending**; **message or theme**. Support every comparison with brief quotation."),
     ("pieges", "Common faults", "- Writing all about poem A, then all about poem B with no links.\n- Treating features as equal when one matters more.\n- Ignoring form and sound.\n- Overlong quotations.")],
    [("Example 1 — Spot similarities and differences", "Original poems on a town. Poem A: ‘The town is a drum that never rests, / its lorries beating on my chest.’ Poem B: ‘Between the stalls the evening glows, / and every stranger greets and goes.’ Compare their view of town life.",
      ["Both use sound and image to present the town.", "A: harsh, noisy, oppressive metaphor (drum, beating on my chest).", "B: warm, friendly mood (glows, greets).", "Similar couplet form but contrasting attitudes: stress versus comfort."], "Both are couplets about the town but A is oppressive and B is warm."),
     ("Example 2 — Write a comparative sentence", "Write one comparative sentence linking the two poems.",
      ["Use a connective and refer to both poems with quotations.", "‘Whereas poem A presents the town as an oppressive ‘drum’, poem B softens it with the warm image of an ‘evening glows’, showing different attitudes to the same setting.’"], "A sentence with connective and two quotations.")],
    [M("In an integrated comparison you", "Discuss both poems within each paragraph", ["Write all of one poem then the other", "Ignore one poem", "Only quote"], "Keep both in view."),
     TF("‘Whereas’ and ‘in contrast’ are comparative connectives.", True, "They signal difference."),
     MT("Match each feature to what you compare.", [("Imagery", "Type and effect of images"), ("Form", "Stanzas, rhyme and rhythm"), ("Tone", "Attitude and shifts"), ("Ending", "Final impression")], "Possible points."),
     M("Which opening best starts a comparison?", "Both poems explore life in a town but present it very differently", ["Poem A has four lines.", "I do not like poems.", "The poets were born in towns."], "It names the shared theme and contrast."),
     O("Original: Poem A ‘The sea is a door that opens in the night’; Poem B ‘The sea is a wall, grey and shut’. Write a comparative paragraph on the sea.", "Both poems use a metaphor for the sea, but they differ in what it suggests. In A the sea is a ‘door’ that ‘opens’, which suggests possibility and mystery; in B it is a ‘wall, grey and shut’, which suggests limits and despair. Whereas A’s verb is active and hopeful, B’s adjectives are static and cold.", "Both poems considered (1); contrasting readings (2); quotations (1)."),
     O("Original pair. Poem X (regular rhyme): ‘We plant the maize, we pray for rain, / we wait, we hope, we plant again.’ Poem Y (free verse): ‘The field is cracked. / I stand. / Nobody speaks.’ Compare how each poem presents hardship.", "Both present hardship in farming, but X uses regular rhyme and repetition (‘we plant’) to show persistent, almost ritual endurance and hope; its pattern suggests an unbroken cycle. Y uses short free-verse lines and silence to show stark despair: the cracked field and the lack of speech suggest defeat. X is resilient, Y is bleak, and the forms enhance each mood.", "Similarity (1); contrast in tone (1); form comparison (1); supported by quotation (1).")],
    [("Integrated comparison means", "Treat both poems together", ["Write about one only", "Avoid quotations", "Copy the poem"], "Direct comparison."),
     ("Similarly signals", "A similarity", ["A difference", "A conclusion only", "A quotation"], "Connective."),
     ("Form includes", "Stanzas, rhyme and rhythm", ["Only the title", "Only the author", "The poet’s age"], "Structure features."),
     ("A comparison should end with", "A judgement about effects", ["A new poem", "A summary of rhyme", "A blank line"], "Evaluation."),
     ("An unseen poem is one", "You have not studied before", ["You wrote", "Without a title", "That is in a prose format"], "Tested skills, not memory.")],
    (flow(["Shared theme and main contrast", "Points: tone, imagery, form, sound", "Both poems in every paragraph", "Judgement on effects"], 440, "blue", 13, 340),
     "Structure of a poetry comparison.", "Four boxes: theme and contrast, points, both poems in each paragraph and a judgement."),
    [NOTE, "All poems are original and short."])

ch0 = ch

L = lesson(ch0, "poetic-voice", "Poetic voice and advanced forms: sonnet and dramatic monologue",
    ["Analyse the speaker, addressee and voice of a poem", "Describe the sonnet and the dramatic monologue", "Explain how a volta (turn) shapes meaning"],
    [("definition", "Speaker and addressee", "The **speaker** (persona) of a poem is not always the poet. A **dramatic monologue** is a poem in which a character speaks to a silent listener and reveals more than they intend, so the reader reads between the lines. Ask: who is speaking, to whom, in what situation, and what does the speaker fail to notice?"),
     ("retenir", "The sonnet", "A **sonnet** has fourteen lines, usually with a rhyme scheme and a **volta** (turn) where the argument or mood shifts. The **Shakespearean** sonnet has three quatrains and a final couplet (ABAB CDCD EFEF GG); the **Petrarchan** has an octave and a sestet. The compact form pushes the poet to concentrate an idea, and the turn changes direction."),
     ("methode", "Analysing voice and form", "Identify the speaker, tone and any **irony** between what is said and what is shown. Note the form, rhyme and where the turn occurs, and explain how the structure supports the meaning, for example by delaying the main idea until the couplet. Use short quotations."),
     ("pieges", "Avoid", "- Assuming that ‘I’ in a poem is the poet.\n- Saying that a sonnet is about love by definition: sonnets treat many themes.\n- Naming the volta without explaining what changes.")],
    [("Example 1 — Dramatic monologue", "Original lines: ‘Of course I am not angry. I merely mention that the fence you built stands one foot on my land; but I am not angry. Please have more tea.’ Comment on the speaker.",
      ["The speaker denies anger while describing a grievance.", "‘I merely mention’ and ‘Please have more tea’ show forced politeness.", "The reader sees irritation beneath the calm: ironic self-revelation."], "Politeness masks anger; the speaker reveals more than intended."),
     ("Example 2 — The turn", "Original sonnet closing lines: ‘For years I called this valley mine alone— / yet now the river sings to every home.’ What does the turn do?",
      ["The ‘yet’ marks the volta.", "It moves from possession (‘mine alone’) to sharing (‘every home’).", "The final couplet gives a gentle resolution."], "It shifts from private ownership to shared belonging.")],
    [M("A dramatic monologue is", "A poem in which a character speaks to a silent listener", ["A poem without a speaker", "A stage direction", "A prose summary"], "Persona and addressee."),
     TF("The speaker of a poem is always the poet.", False, "A persona may be created."),
     MT("Match the term to the meaning.", [("Volta", "Turn in thought or tone"), ("Octave", "First eight lines of a Petrarchan sonnet"), ("Persona", "Speaker created by the poet"), ("Couplet", "Two rhyming lines")], "Standard terms."),
     M("The Shakespearean sonnet ends with", "A rhyming couplet", ["A refrain", "A haiku", "A stage direction"], "GG."),
     O("Original lines: ‘I have never been jealous. It is only that the neighbours’ roof seems taller than mine every morning.’ Explain what the speaker reveals.", "The denial of jealousy is contradicted by the careful comparison of roofs, so the speaker reveals envy and insecurity. The humour comes from the gap between what is said and what is shown.", "Denial and contradiction (1); revelation (1); tone (1); irony (1)."),
     O("Original sonnet couplet-ending: ‘And so the clock keeps time, but I keep none; / the evening comes, and finds me almost done.’ Discuss how the final couplet might work as a conclusion.", "The couplet’s rhyme (none/done) gives closure and a sense of resignation. The clock imagery contrasts mechanical time with the speaker’s lack of control, so the ending expresses acceptance and a note of weariness. It resolves the poem’s tension quietly.", "Rhyme and closure (1); imagery (1); tone (1); effect (1).")],
    [("A volta marks", "A turn in the poem", ["The title", "The refrain", "The end of a stanza only"], "Shift of thought."),
     ("A sonnet has", "Fourteen lines", ["Twelve lines", "Four lines", "Three lines"], "Standard."),
     ("A persona is", "A speaker created by the poet", ["The poet’s name", "A stanza", "A rhyme"], "Not always the poet."),
     ("Irony in a dramatic monologue arises when", "The speaker reveals more than he or she intends", ["The poem rhymes", "The poem is short", "There is no speaker"], "Reader reads between the lines."),
     ("The Petrarchan sonnet has", "An octave and a sestet", ["Three quatrains and a couplet", "A single stanza of ten lines", "Seven couplets"], "Eight plus six lines.")],
    (table(["Sonnet type", "Structure"], [["Shakespearean", "3 quatrains + couplet"], ["Petrarchan", "Octave + sestet"]], 440, 13, 36),
     "Two common sonnet structures.", "A table with the Shakespearean and Petrarchan sonnet structures."),
    [NOTE, "Sonnet names and structures are standard; check the syllabus for named poets or forms."])

L = lesson(ch0, "context-reader", "Context, audience and the reader",
    ["Explain how historical and social context can inform a reading", "Distinguish context from interpretation", "Use context economically in an essay"],
    [("definition", "What is context?", "**Context** is the setting in which a text is written and read: historical events, social attitudes, culture, literary tradition, the author’s life and the audience. Context can **illuminate** a text, but the essay must stay about the text’s words. Distinguish the **context of writing** from the **context of reception** (how readers today respond)."),
     ("retenir", "Using context well", "Make context **relevant** and **brief**: one or two sentences linked to a specific detail in the text. Show how the context affects meaning (for example, a text written under strict social rules may use indirect speech). Avoid biographical guesses and unverified claims."),
     ("retenir", "The reader", "Readers bring their own experience, so interpretations differ. A good essay allows for **more than one reading**, supports each with evidence and decides which is most convincing. Awareness of audience and purpose (to entertain, to persuade, to criticise) also helps."),
     ("pieges", "Pitfalls", "- Dropping historical facts without linking them to the text.\n- Making unverified claims about an author’s life.\n- Treating context as the whole answer.\n- Forgetting that readers today may respond differently.")],
    [("Example 1 — Relevant context", "Original text: a story from an imaginary society where young people must ask elders’ permission to speak. A character says, ‘If it is allowed, I would like to be silent.’ How might context help?",
      ["Knowing that speaking requires permission explains the polite phrasing.", "It shows that silence may be a protest or a safe choice.", "Context illuminates tone but the reading must rest on the words."], "The social rule explains the indirect speech and gives the line irony."),
     ("Example 2 — Two readings", "Original sentence: ‘The new school stood tall, and the old tree fell for it.’ Give two readings.",
      ["Reading 1: progress replaces nature; the tree is a sacrifice.", "Reading 2: the tree ‘fell for’ the school like someone falling in love: humour and affection for the community’s change.", "Both rest on wordplay: ‘fell for it’."], "Progress versus loss, or playful affection through wordplay.")],
    [M("Context means", "The background in which a text is written or read", ["Only the title", "Only the rhyme", "The paper"], "Historical and social background."),
     TF("Context should be linked to specific details in the text.", True, "Otherwise it is only background."),
     MT("Match the term to the meaning.", [("Context of writing", "Conditions when the text was produced"), ("Context of reception", "How readers respond over time"), ("Audience", "Intended readers"), ("Purpose", "Aim of the text")], "Distinctions."),
     M("A weak use of context is", "Listing facts without linking them to the text", ["Linking to a quotation", "Explaining an effect", "Keeping it brief"], "Needs relevance."),
     O("Explain why a reader today might respond differently to a text from the past.", "Readers bring different values, knowledge and experience, and social attitudes change. Aspects of the text that seemed normal at the time may now seem unusual or unjust, while details that mattered then may be less clear now.", "Different values (1); change over time (1); example (1); effect on reading (1)."),
     O("Original sentence from a story: ‘The telegram arrived on market day, and no one dared open it before the drums began.’ Write a short paragraph using context sensibly to comment on the sentence.", "The telegram suggests a time when news came slowly and often meant trouble, and the delay until the drums began shows a community custom of gathering before receiving serious news. The phrase ‘no one dared’ creates tension and suggests the weight of the unknown. Context helps explain the ritual but the effect comes from the writer’s choice of words.", "Context linked to detail (1); language (1); effect (1); balance (1).")],
    [("Context of reception refers to", "How readers respond over time", ["How the text was printed", "The size of the book", "The title page"], "Different eras, different reactions."),
     ("Relevant context is", "Linked to specific details in the text", ["Copied from a book", "Unrelated facts", "A summary of the author’s life only"], "Needs integration."),
     ("An essay should allow for", "More than one reading", ["Only one reading", "No reading", "A rhyme"], "Support each with evidence."),
     ("Purpose refers to", "The aim of the text", ["Its price", "Its font", "Its paper"], "To entertain, persuade or criticise."),
     ("Unverified claims about an author’s life should be", "Avoided", ["Repeated", "Used as main evidence", "Added to every answer"], "Accuracy matters.")],
    (flow(["Text: close reading of words", "Context: brief and relevant link", "Reader: allow more than one reading", "Judgement: which reading is most convincing"], 440, "blue", 13, 340),
     "Using context and the reader in an essay.", "Four boxes: text, context, reader and judgement."),
    [NOTE, "Avoid biographical claims about authors; an expert should add set-text contexts."])

# ====================================================== DRAMA, AFRICAN LIT, THEORY
ch = p.chapter("drama-african", "Drama in performance and African literature")
L = lesson(ch, "staging", "Staging and performance",
    ["Analyse how stagecraft creates meaning", "Discuss directorial choices", "Comment on a scene as a performance"],
    [("definition", "Elements of stagecraft", "**Set and props** show place and status; **lighting** suggests time and mood; **costume** signals character; **sound and music** build atmosphere; **blocking** is the actors’ movement and positions; **proxemics** is the distance between actors; **pace and silence** control tension. Directors choose how to use these elements to interpret a play."),
     ("retenir", "Language and action", "Playwrights use **stage directions**, dialogue rhythm, **soliloquy** and **dramatic irony** to guide performance. Entrances and exits, **levels** (high and low positions), and **symbolic objects** carry meaning. In African performance traditions, drumming, song, dance and audience participation may be integrated with spoken drama."),
     ("methode", "Discussing a scene in performance", "Describe a **choice** (for example the actor stands upstage and does not move), explain the **effect** (power, isolation) and link it to **theme or character**. Offer alternatives: ‘A director might…’. Support from the text with brief quotations and stage directions."),
     ("attention", "Do not forget", "- You are analysing performance, not just reading words.\n- Justify each choice from the text.\n- Do not describe a production you have not seen as if it were fact: propose choices.")],
    [("Example 1 — Blocking", "Original scene: a chief (seated, centre stage) listens to a young woman (standing, downstage) who asks to speak. Comment on the staging.",
      ["The chief is seated centre and elevated: authority and power.", "The young woman stands downstage and closer to the audience: a vulnerable but sympathetic position.", "The distance between them suggests tension and respect."], "Positions show power and invite audience sympathy for the young woman."),
     ("Example 2 — Lighting", "Original: at the climax of a scene, the stage lights slowly dim except for a spot on a locked door. What is the effect?",
      ["The narrowing light focuses attention on the door.", "It creates tension and suggests that the door holds an important secret.", "The surrounding darkness isolates the characters and increases suspense."], "Focus on the door builds suspense.")],
    [M("Blocking refers to", "The movement and positions of actors", ["The ending of a play", "The script’s rhyme", "The seats"], "Part of staging."),
     TF("A director may interpret a scene through lighting, costume and sound.", True, "These are elements of stagecraft."),
     MT("Match the element to its effect.", [("Lighting", "Sets mood and focus"), ("Costume", "Signals character or status"), ("Sound", "Builds atmosphere"), ("Silence", "Creates tension")], "Standard effects."),
     M("Proxemics concerns", "Distance between actors", ["Rhyme scheme", "The script length", "Tickets"], "Space between characters."),
     O("Original stage direction: ‘(She turns her back to him and speaks to the audience.)’ Discuss the effect.", "Turning her back signals rejection or private feeling, while speaking to the audience creates intimacy and shared knowledge, like an aside. The audience is drawn to side with her and may feel the tension with the other character.", "Gesture (1); audience effect (1); character (1); technique named (1)."),
     O("Original scene: A mother kneels at the door, an unopened letter on the table, while her son is heard singing off stage. Suggest how you would stage the moment and explain the effect.", "I would keep the mother low and still, with a pool of light on her and the letter, while the son’s song continues faintly from off stage. The contrast between his happiness and her stillness would create dramatic irony and sadness, as the audience senses that the letter will change the family’s life. Silence after the song stops would heighten the tension.", "Staging choices (2); effect on audience (1); link to theme/irony (1).")],
    [("Levels in staging are", "High and low positions", ["Pages", "Poems", "Chapters"], "Used to show power."),
     ("A prop is", "An object used on stage", ["A kind of rhyme", "A stage manager", "The audience"], "E.g. a letter."),
     ("Upstage is", "Away from the audience", ["Closest to audience", "Outside", "Backstage only"], "Back of stage."),
     ("Spotlighting an object", "Focuses attention on it", ["Hides it", "Changes the script", "Ends the play"], "Directs the audience."),
     ("An aside is", "A remark to the audience", ["A scene change", "A rhyme", "A prop"], "Direct address.")],
    (shapes([RECT(40, 20, 360, 160, stroke="ink", width=2), T(220, 45, "Upstage (far from audience)", 12), T(220, 165, "Downstage (near the audience)", 12), T(60, 110, "Left", 12, anchor="start"), T(380, 110, "Right", 12, anchor="end"), CIRCLE(220, 95, 14, fill="orange"), T(220, 100, "A", 12, bold=True)], 440, 200),
     "A stage plan with the audience below.", "A rectangle representing a stage with upstage at the top, downstage at the bottom, and an actor marked A at the centre."),
    [NOTE, "Stage-direction terms (upstage, downstage) are standard; check whether the syllabus needs stage left/right conventions."])

L = lesson(ch, "african-themes", "African literature: oral roots and themes (general)",
    ["Describe common concerns of African writing in general terms", "Explain the relation between oral tradition and written literature", "Analyse an original extract on tradition and change"],
    [("definition", "General concerns", "African literature is written in many languages and genres. Common concerns across much modern writing include **colonialism and its legacy**, **identity**, **tradition versus modernity**, **language choice** (indigenous languages or European languages), **migration and the city**, **gender roles**, **power and corruption after independence**, **community and family**. Treat these as general themes: each text is different."),
     ("retenir", "Oral roots", "Many writers draw on oral forms: proverbs, folktale structure, praise names, song and a storytelling voice. This can give a text its rhythm, humour and moral frame. Critics ask how an author moves between **oral and written** modes and what languages they use, and whether the text addresses local or international readers."),
     ("retenir", "Analysing themes", "Link a theme to **characters** (who embodies tradition or change?), **setting** (village and city), **language** (proverb, idiom, translation), and **form** (narrator, structure). Show **complexity**: texts rarely present tradition as wholly good or modernity as wholly bad, or the reverse."),
     ("attention", "Care and accuracy", "- Do not generalise about ‘all African writers’ or make claims about particular authors without checking.\n- Avoid stereotypes: Africa is diverse.\n- Refer to the texts you have studied, not to assumptions.")],
    [("Example 1 — Theme in an extract", "Original extract: ‘Nkeng returned from the city in a bright suit and a worry she could not fold away. Her grandmother greeted her in the old language and watched her answer in English, then in a mixture of both. “You are still mine,” she said, “even in a borrowed tongue.”’ Identify two themes.",
      ["Language choice and mixture: identity.", "Tradition versus modernity: the grandmother and city life.", "Belonging: ‘still mine’ affirms family ties despite change."], "Identity and language; tradition and change."),
     ("Example 2 — Oral features in writing", "Original sentence: ‘As our people say, the pot that is hurried never boils the yam well.’ What oral feature appears?",
      ["A proverb (original) opening with ‘As our people say’.", "The proverb gives a communal moral voice.", "Such devices tie the written narrative to oral tradition."], "A proverb that evokes communal voice.")],
    [M("Which is a common theme in much African writing?", "Tradition and modernity", ["Space travel only", "Weather reports", "Cooking recipes only"], "A general concern."),
     TF("Oral tradition may influence written African literature.", True, "Many writers use oral forms."),
     MT("Match the concern with an example question.", [("Identity", "Who am I between two cultures?"), ("Language choice", "Which language should be used to write?"), ("Power", "How do leaders use authority?"), ("Gender", "What roles are open to women and men?")], "General concerns."),
     M("When writing about African literature, a student should avoid", "Generalising about all African writers", ["Quoting the text", "Discussing themes", "Comparing texts"], "Be careful and specific."),
     O("Original sentence: ‘The village elders praised him, but the city men measured him by his phone.’ Comment on what it suggests about values.", "It contrasts two systems of value: the elders’ respect based on character and community and the city men’s judgement by possessions. The sentence suggests the pull between tradition and modern materialism.", "Contrast identified (1); two value systems (1); effect (1); quotation (1)."),
     O("Original passage: ‘My father’s stories were told under the mango tree, in a language my teacher called a dialect. I wrote my first poem in the teacher’s language and felt like a guest at my own table.’ Write a paragraph on how the passage presents language and identity.", "The passage contrasts the father’s oral stories in the home language with the teacher’s dismissive label ‘dialect’, suggesting a hierarchy of languages. The simile-like ‘guest at my own table’ shows alienation when writing in the school language. The speaker is torn between belonging and success. The passage presents language as bound up with identity and power.", "Contrast (1); technique or image (1); effect on identity (1); link to theme (1).")],
    [("Tradition versus modernity is", "A common theme", ["Never discussed", "A rhyme", "A stage prop"], "A general concern."),
     ("A proverb in a novel can", "Give a communal, oral voice", ["End the book", "Replace the plot", "Be a title"], "A link to oral tradition."),
     ("Language choice concerns", "Which language to write in", ["Only spelling", "Only paper", "Only titles"], "A major discussion."),
     ("The best approach to a theme is to", "Show complexity with textual evidence", ["Make a stereotype", "Avoid quotations", "Ignore the text"], "Nuance."),
     ("Oral and written literature", "Can influence each other", ["Never interact", "Are identical", "Are unrelated"], "Writers draw on oral forms.")],
    (table(["Concern", "Question"], [["Identity", "Who am I?"], ["Tradition/modernity", "What is kept, what changes?"], ["Language", "Which tongue to use?"], ["Power", "Who rules, how?"]], 440, 13, 32),
     "General concerns in African writing.", "A table with four concerns and a guiding question for each."),
    [NOTE, "No claims about specific authors or works are made; an expert must add set-text-specific content and check generalisations."])

# ====================================================== THEORY
ch = p.chapter("theory", "Critical approaches and the extended essay")
L = lesson(ch, "postcolonial", "Post-colonial criticism (outline)",
    ["Define key ideas of post-colonial criticism", "Apply them to an original passage", "Discuss strengths and limits of the approach"],
    [("definition", "Key ideas", "**Post-colonial criticism** studies how literature deals with colonialism and its after-effects. Key ideas: **centre and margin**; the colonised as the **‘Other’** in colonial writing (discussed by Edward Said); **hybridity**, the mixing of cultures (Homi Bhabha); **language and power**; **resistance** and **writing back**, where writers answer colonial representations; and **decolonising** the canon."),
     ("retenir", "Applying the approach", "Ask: Who speaks and who is silent? How are places and peoples described? What does language choice imply? How does the text show cultural mixing, loss or resistance? Does the text accept or challenge colonial stereotypes? Always ground the reading in specific words."),
     ("retenir", "Strengths and limits", "**Strengths:** highlights power and history; makes visible silenced voices. **Limits:** may reduce a text to politics; ‘post-colonial’ covers very different regions and experiences; must avoid ignoring aesthetic qualities, individual authors and non-political themes."),
     ("pieges", "Common errors", "- Applying theory without evidence from the text.\n- Treating all colonised societies as the same.\n- Assuming a text has only one political meaning.\n- Misusing terms: ‘hybridity’ is not simply ‘mixture’ but a creative blending with power implications.")],
    [("Example 1 — Apply the ‘Other’", "Original passage from an imaginary traveller’s diary: ‘The villagers were strange and silent, a part of the landscape, like the trees. They did not seem to have thoughts of their own.’ Analyse using post-colonial ideas.",
      ["The traveller treats the villagers as an ‘Other’, part of the landscape.", "The comparison with trees denies individuality.", "The diary assumes a colonial viewpoint and silences their voices; a post-colonial reading would expose this bias."], "A colonial gaze presenting people as an object and ‘Other’."),
     ("Example 2 — Writing back", "Original: a poem titled ‘Reply to the Diary’ gives the villagers’ own names, voices and humour. How does it ‘write back’?",
      ["It challenges the earlier silence and stereotype.", "Naming gives individuality.", "Humour and voice reclaim narrative control."], "It counters stereotypes by giving voice and names.")],
    [M("‘The Other’ in post-colonial theory refers to", "A group represented as different and inferior", ["A famous author", "A rhyme", "A printing term"], "Colonial representation."),
     TF("Hybridity refers to the mixing of cultures.", True, "Discussed by Homi Bhabha."),
     MT("Match the idea to the meaning.", [("Writing back", "Answering colonial representations"), ("Centre and margin", "Unequal power between places"), ("Language and power", "Which language has status"), ("Resistance", "Challenging colonial control")], "Key ideas."),
     M("A limit of the post-colonial approach is that it may", "Reduce a text to politics", ["Ignore history", "Help the reader", "Make quotations unnecessary"], "Balance is needed."),
     O("Explain the term ‘writing back’.", "Writing back means that formerly colonised writers respond to colonial literature and stereotypes by giving their own versions, voices and perspectives, challenging earlier representations and reclaiming the right to tell their stories.", "Definition (1); idea of response (1); challenging stereotypes (1); example (1)."),
     O("Original passage: ‘The visitors named our river for their queen and called us a tribe. We kept calling it Mother and calling ourselves by our own names.’ Write a paragraph applying post-colonial criticism.", "The passage shows naming as an act of power: the visitors rename the river and label the people, imposing their view. The speaker’s resistance is quiet but strong, as continuing to say ‘Mother’ and ‘our own names’ preserves identity and cultural memory. The contrast between the two names shows the conflict between colonial and local ways of seeing, and the repeated ‘our’ reasserts community.", "Naming as power (1); resistance (1); language or technique (1); link to theory (1).")],
    [("Edward Said’s work concerns", "Representations of the ‘Orient’ and the ‘Other’", ["Rhyme schemes", "Stage design", "Haiku"], "A founding text of the field."),
     ("Hybridity is associated with", "Homi Bhabha", ["Aristotle", "Bismarck", "Livingstone"], "Cultural mixing."),
     ("Post-colonial criticism asks", "Whose voice is heard and whose is silenced", ["How long the title is", "Which paper is used", "How many pages"], "Power and voice."),
     ("A weakness is", "Reading everything only as politics", ["Reading the text carefully", "Using quotations", "Comparing texts"], "Balance."),
     ("‘Decolonising the canon’ means", "Broadening which texts are valued", ["Burning books", "Ending literature", "Removing poems"], "Widening and questioning.")],
    (flow(["Whose voice is heard?", "How are people and places described?", "What does language imply?", "Where is resistance or hybridity?"], 440, "blue", 13, 320),
     "Questions to ask in a post-colonial reading.", "Four boxes: voice, description, language and resistance or hybridity."),
    [NOTE, "Theorists named (Said, Bhabha) for general orientation: confirm which critics and terms the syllabus requires; check summaries of their ideas."])

L = lesson(ch, "feminist-formalist", "Feminist, formalist and other critical lenses",
    ["Define feminist criticism and formalism in outline", "Apply two approaches to the same original passage", "Compare and evaluate approaches"],
    [("definition", "Feminist criticism", "**Feminist criticism** examines how literature represents **gender**, how women and men are shown, whose voice is heard, and how **patriarchy** (male dominance) shapes the text and its reception. It asks about stereotypes, silence, agency and whether the text reinforces or challenges gender roles. Writers often cited include Virginia Woolf and Simone de Beauvoir for general ideas."),
     ("retenir", "Formalism", "**Formalism** (including New Criticism) focuses on the **text itself**: form, structure, language, imagery, irony and ambiguity, and how they work together. Biography and history are secondary. The method is **close reading**: a careful analysis of how the words create meaning."),
     ("retenir", "Other lenses", "**Reader-response** studies how readers create meaning. **Marxist** criticism examines class and economic power. **Historical or biographical** approaches look at context. Different approaches highlight different features, and a strong essay may combine them while staying focused on the text."),
     ("pieges", "Avoid", "- Using theory as a label rather than as a method with evidence.\n- Assuming feminist criticism is only about women characters: it also considers masculinity and language.\n- Treating formalism as ‘ignoring meaning’: it seeks meaning through form.")],
    [("Example 1 — Feminist reading", "Original passage: ‘Maria served the guests without sitting. The men’s voices rose around her; she was asked for more water, and no one asked her opinion.’ Give a feminist reading.",
      ["Maria works while others talk: a gendered division of tasks.", "‘No one asked her opinion’ shows silence and lack of agency.", "The passage may highlight the unequal voices in the household."], "It exposes inequality of voice and labour."),
     ("Example 2 — Formalist reading", "Use the same passage for a formalist reading.",
      ["Form: simple sentences and repetition of serving actions create a steady rhythm.", "The contrast between ‘voices rose’ and her silence is built through juxtaposition.", "The structure mirrors her position at the edge of the conversation."], "Rhythm, contrast and structure convey her marginal position.")],
    [M("Formalism focuses mainly on", "The text’s form and language", ["The author’s biography", "The reader’s age", "The publishing date"], "Close reading."),
     TF("Feminist criticism considers how gender is represented in a text.", True, "Definition."),
     MT("Match the lens to its focus.", [("Marxist", "Class and economic power"), ("Reader-response", "How readers create meaning"), ("Formalist", "Form and language"), ("Feminist", "Gender and voice")], "Basic lenses."),
     M("Close reading means", "Careful analysis of language and form", ["Reading quickly", "Ignoring details", "Summarising only"], "Core of formalism."),
     O("Explain one advantage and one limitation of formalism.", "Advantage: it encourages careful attention to the text’s language and structure, which gives evidence for interpretation. Limitation: it can ignore historical, social and personal contexts that help explain meaning.", "Advantage (2); limitation (2)."),
     O("Original passage: ‘The headmaster spoke; the teachers wrote. When Miss Atem raised a point, the headmaster smiled and continued as before.’ Apply two critical approaches to the passage and compare what each reveals.", "A feminist reading sees Miss Atem’s point being ignored with a smile as a sign of how male authority dismisses a woman’s voice. A formalist reading focuses on the sentence structure: the short, parallel clauses (‘spoke’, ‘wrote’) establish a hierarchy, and ‘smiled and continued as before’ gives a calm, understated irony. The feminist approach connects the text to gender and power, the formalist approach shows how the writing creates the effect; together they give a fuller reading.", "Feminist reading (2); formalist reading (2); comparison (1).")],
    [("Patriarchy refers to", "Male dominance in society", ["A type of poem", "A theatre term", "A rhyme scheme"], "Used in feminist criticism."),
     ("New Criticism is a form of", "Formalism", ["Marxism", "Reader-response", "Feminism"], "Focus on the text."),
     ("Reader-response criticism looks at", "How readers create meaning", ["Printing history", "Authors’ ages", "Rhyme counts"], "Role of the reader."),
     ("Marxist criticism examines", "Class and economic power", ["Rhythm only", "Titles", "Paper"], "Material conditions."),
     ("A strong essay using theory should", "Support it with textual evidence", ["Avoid the text", "Use only labels", "Skip quotations"], "Theory is a tool.")],
    (table(["Lens", "Asks"], [["Feminist", "Whose voice and what role?"], ["Formalist", "How do form and words work?"], ["Marxist", "Who holds economic power?"], ["Reader-response", "How do readers make meaning?"]], 440, 12, 32),
     "Four critical lenses and their questions.", "A table with four critical lenses and the key question of each."),
    [NOTE, "Theory summaries are outlines: check which approaches and critics the syllabus names."])

L = lesson(ch, "irony-satire", "Irony, satire and humour",
    ["Distinguish types of irony and satire", "Analyse how satire criticises society", "Write about tone in an original satirical extract"],
    [("definition", "Irony and satire", "**Verbal irony** says the opposite of what is meant; **situational irony** is an outcome contrary to expectation; **dramatic irony** is when the audience knows more than the characters. **Satire** uses humour, exaggeration and irony to criticise society, institutions or behaviour, often hoping to reform them. **Parody** imitates a style to mock it; **caricature** exaggerates features."),
     ("retenir", "Techniques", "**Exaggeration**, **understatement**, **mock-seriousness** (a calm tone about an absurd subject), **juxtaposition** (placing contrasting things together), **irony** and **absurd logic**. Satire can be gentle (Horatian) or sharp (Juvenalian). The target and the writer’s tone should be identified."),
     ("methode", "Writing about satire", "Identify the **target** (what is criticised), the **technique**, the **tone** and the **effect** on the reader. Ask: is the humour warm or bitter? What would the writer want us to change or think? Always support with quotation."),
     ("attention", "Mistakes to avoid", "- Treating satire as only funny: it has a serious aim.\n- Ignoring the target of the satire.\n- Reading ironic statements literally.")],
    [("Example 1 — Identify the target", "Original extract: ‘The Ministry of Delays announced that the new road would be finished in record time, namely, next to the previous record, which was also next year.’ Identify target and technique.",
      ["Target: officials’ unfulfilled promises.", "Technique: the ironic name ‘Ministry of Delays’, and the absurd logic of ‘record time’.", "Tone: dry, mock-serious."], "Satire of broken promises through irony and absurd logic."),
     ("Example 2 — Effect of exaggeration", "Original: ‘The chairman spoke for so long that two of the clocks left the room in protest.’ Comment on the effect.",
      ["Hyperbole: clocks cannot leave the room.", "Personification makes the clocks critics.", "Effect: comic mockery of long-winded speech."], "Humorous criticism through hyperbole and personification.")],
    [M("Satire uses humour mainly to", "Criticise society or behaviour", ["Praise everything", "Avoid meaning", "Teach rhyme"], "It has a critical aim."),
     TF("Understatement can be a satirical technique.", True, "It can create irony."),
     MT("Match the term to the meaning.", [("Parody", "Imitation to mock"), ("Caricature", "Exaggerated portrayal"), ("Verbal irony", "Saying the opposite"), ("Hyperbole", "Extreme exaggeration")], "Definitions."),
     M("The target of satire is", "What is being criticised", ["The rhyme", "The reader’s age", "The printer"], "Identify it."),
     O("Original sentence: ‘The mayor, who had never missed a free lunch, gave a stirring speech on the dangers of waste.’ Explain the irony.", "The mayor’s record of free lunches contradicts his speech against waste, creating situational or verbal irony and exposing hypocrisy. The tone is dry and mocking.", "Irony identified (1); contradiction (1); target (1); tone (1)."),
     O("Original extract: ‘At the Society for Punctuality the meeting began, as always, an hour after the notice. The secretary apologised for the early start.’ Write a paragraph on how satire works here.", "The satire targets those who claim to value punctuality but are always late. The society’s name sets up an expectation that the details contradict. The secretary’s apology for the ‘early’ start is a comic reversal that exaggerates the group’s lateness. The deadpan tone makes the criticism gentle but sharp, inviting us to laugh at hypocrisy.", "Target (1); irony or reversal (1); tone (1); effect (1).")],
    [("Parody", "Imitates a style to mock it", ["Praises a style", "Is a rhyme", "Is a stage"], "Humorous imitation."),
     ("Mock-seriousness means", "Speaking calmly about something absurd", ["Shouting", "Rhyming", "Avoiding humour"], "Deadpan."),
     ("Juxtaposition is", "Placing contrasting things side by side", ["A rhyme", "A title", "A line break only"], "Creates irony or contrast."),
     ("Satire often aims to", "Encourage reform or reflection", ["Hide ideas", "End debate", "Avoid society"], "Serious purpose."),
     ("Caricature", "Exaggerates features", ["Hides traits", "Is a quotation", "Is a rhyme"], "Used in satire.")],
    (flow(["Target: what is criticised?", "Technique: irony, exaggeration, parody", "Tone: gentle or bitter", "Effect: laughter and a message"], 440, "blue", 13, 320),
     "Analysing satire in four steps.", "Four boxes: target, technique, tone and effect."),
    [NOTE, "All satirical extracts are original; avoid references to real people or institutions."])

L = lesson(ch, "comparison-essay", "The comparison and critical essay",
    ["Plan and write a comparison of two texts or extracts", "Build a sustained argument with counter-argument", "Manage time and write a polished conclusion"],
    [("methode", "Plan a comparison", "Choose 3–4 **points of comparison** that fit the question (for example attitude to authority, narrative voice, language, structure, ending). For each point, note **both texts**: similarity, difference, evidence. Decide which comparison matters most and let it drive your **thesis**."),
     ("retenir", "Structure and language", "Prefer an **integrated** structure (point by point) to a block structure (text A then text B). Use comparative language: *similarly, likewise, in contrast, whereas, while, unlike*. Introduce a **counter-argument** (‘It could be argued…’) and respond to it. Make a **judgement** in your conclusion: which text more effectively presents the theme, and why?"),
     ("retenir", "Quality markers", "A top essay is **focused** on the question, **analytical** (technique and effect), **evidence-based** with short quotations, **comparative throughout**, **well-organised** and **fluent**. It shows awareness of **context** and **critical approaches** where relevant, without letting them replace close reading."),
     ("pieges", "Pitfalls", "- Treating the two texts separately.\n- Retelling plot.\n- A conclusion that only repeats the introduction.\n- Over-using theory labels without evidence.")],
    [("Example 1 — Build a thesis", "Question: ‘Compare how two writers present the tension between tradition and change.’ Original texts: A (a poem in which a grandmother keeps an old drum), B (a prose extract in which a son sells his father’s drum). Write a thesis.",
      ["Both texts use the drum as a symbol.", "A presents tradition as precious and fragile; B shows how economic pressure erodes it.", "Thesis: while both writers use the drum as a symbol of heritage, A emphasises preservation and B shows the cost of change."], "Shared symbol, different emphasis."),
     ("Example 2 — Write a comparison paragraph", "Write the first point of comparison for the thesis above.",
      ["Point: both texts make the drum carry emotional weight.", "A: ‘she wipes the drum like a sleeping child’ (imaginary) shows tenderness.", "B: ‘it sat in the shop, a price tag on its hide’ shows commodification.", "Contrast: affection versus trade."], "Both use the drum as symbol but with different tones.")],
    [M("An integrated comparison structure is", "Point by point, with both texts in each paragraph", ["Text A first, then text B afterwards", "Only one text", "A list of quotations"], "Direct comparison."),
     TF("A counter-argument can strengthen an essay.", True, "It shows balance."),
     MT("Match the connective to its use.", [("Similarly", "Shows similarity"), ("Whereas", "Shows contrast"), ("It could be argued", "Introduces a counter-argument"), ("Therefore", "Introduces a conclusion")], "Useful connectives."),
     M("A strong conclusion", "Makes a reasoned judgement", ["Repeats the plot", "Adds new quotations", "Is omitted"], "Evaluation."),
     O("Write a thesis for: ‘Compare the ways in which two poems present the city.’ (Poem A is noisy and oppressive; poem B is warm and welcoming.)", "Although both poems use sensory imagery of the city, A presents it as an overwhelming force through harsh sound images, while B presents it as a place of human connection through warm images.", "Both poems named (1); contrast (1); focus on technique (1); clear wording (1)."),
     O("Plan (not write in full) an essay on: ‘Compare the presentation of conflict between generations in two extracts.’ Use imaginary extracts: extract X shows a father and daughter arguing over school; extract Y shows a mother silently leaving a room.", "Thesis: both extracts show generational conflict, but X is open and verbal while Y is silent and indirect. Para 1: dialogue and tone (X loud and direct; Y silence). Para 2: stage directions or narrative techniques (X reported speech; Y withheld thoughts). Para 3: outcome and message (X reconciliation possible; Y lingering tension). Counter-argument: silence can also be a form of strong speech. Conclusion: judgement that Y’s silence may be more powerful because of what it implies.", "Thesis (1); integrated points (2); counter-argument (1); conclusion (1).")],
    [("Block structure means", "Writing about one text then the other", ["Mixing both throughout", "Using only quotations", "Avoiding evidence"], "Less effective than integrated."),
     ("‘Unlike’ is a", "Contrastive connective", ["Similarity marker", "Rhyme", "Stage direction"], "Signals difference."),
     ("A good thesis", "Answers the question directly", ["Is a plot summary", "Is a quotation", "Is a title"], "Line of argument."),
     ("Counter-arguments", "Show balanced thinking", ["Weaken the essay always", "Replace the thesis", "Are forbidden"], "Respond to them."),
     ("Context in an essay should", "Support, not replace, close reading", ["Replace analysis", "Be avoided", "Be copied"], "Balance.")],
    (flow(["Choose 3–4 comparison points", "Both texts in every paragraph", "Counter-argument and response", "Judgement in the conclusion"], 440, "blue", 13, 340),
     "A comparison essay in four steps.", "Four boxes: comparison points, both texts, counter-argument and judgement."),
    [NOTE, "Quality markers are indicative: check the official GCE Board mark scheme for assessment objectives."])

# ====================================================== MOCK
poem = ("Original poem — ‘Market Road’:\nThe road to market wakes before the sun,\nits dust a bright and busy ghost of feet;\nthe baskets sway, the early songs are begun,\nand every stone remembers where we meet.\nBy noon the heat has quietened every stall;\nthe road lies empty, patient, long and clear;\nthen evening brings us home, and one by one\nwe leave our footprints for the dawn to hear.")
q = mock_fill(L, [
    (M("‘The wind whispered through the grass’ is an example of", "Personification", ["Simile", "Hyperbole", "Oxymoron"], "The wind is given a human action."), 1),
    (M("A soliloquy is", "A character speaking thoughts aloud, alone", ["A remark to the audience only", "A scene change", "A rhyme"], "Reveals inner thoughts."), 1),
    (M("In post-colonial criticism, ‘writing back’ means", "Answering colonial representations from a local perspective", ["Copying colonial texts", "Writing in rhyme", "Ending a novel"], "Reclaiming the narrative."), 1),
    (M("Which approach focuses mainly on the text’s form and language?", "Formalism", ["Marxism", "Reader-response", "Biographical criticism"], "Close reading."), 1),
    (TF("An integrated comparison discusses both texts within each paragraph.", True, "That is the definition."), 1),
    (TF("Satire has no serious purpose.", False, "It criticises through humour."), 1),
    (MT("Match the term to the meaning.", [("Hamartia", "Flaw or error in a tragic hero"), ("Enjambment", "A sentence running over the line end"), ("Dramatic irony", "Audience knows more than the character"), ("Hybridity", "Mixing of cultures")], "Standard terms."), 2),
    (P(poem, [pm("The rhyme scheme of the first quatrain is", "ABAB", ["AABB", "ABBA", "ABCD"], "feet/meet and sun/begun rhyme alternately, giving ABAB.", 1), pm("‘its dust a bright and busy ghost of feet’ is an example of", "Metaphor", ["Simile", "Onomatopoeia", "Alliteration only"], "The dust is said to be a ghost.", 1), po("Describe the tone of the poem and the effect of the road being ‘empty, patient’ at noon.", "The tone is quiet, reflective and gently affectionate. The personified road is ‘patient’ and ‘empty’ at noon, suggesting rest and the rhythm of the day, and giving the road a calm presence that contrasts with its busy morning.", "Tone (1); effect of the images (1).", 2), po("How does the poet use the cycle of the day to present the road as part of community life?", "The poem moves from dawn through noon to evening, so the road follows a daily cycle. At dawn it is busy with feet and songs, at noon it rests, and at night the people’s ‘footprints’ are left for dawn: the road holds the community’s memory and connects generations.", "Structure of the day (1); idea of memory/community (1).", 2)]), None),
    (O("To what extent is the oral tradition important to African written literature? Refer in general terms to features such as proverbs, storytelling voice and song.", "Oral tradition gives many writers forms, rhythms and values: proverbs lend communal wisdom, a storytelling voice invites the audience, and songs add musicality. It shapes structure and humour. But written literature also draws on other influences, such as colonial education, global genres and individual experience. Oral tradition is important but not the only source.", "Oral features (1); other influences (1); judgement (1)."), 3),
    (O("‘A satirical writer must be bitter to be effective.’ Discuss with reference to techniques of satire.", "Bitter satire, with sharp irony, can be powerful and shocking. But gentle satire using humour, understatement and mock-seriousness can also criticise effectively and may persuade readers more easily because it invites them to laugh with the writer. Effectiveness depends on target, tone and audience, so bitterness is not necessary.", "Argument for bitterness (1); argument against (1); judgement (1)."), 3),
])
